rules_version = '2';

// The project has NO Storage bucket: the Cloud Storage for Firebase API has
// never been enabled on `aixcam`, and the project contains zero GCS buckets.
// (The STORAGE_BUCKET key in GoogleService-Info.plist names
// aixcam.firebasestorage.app, but that string is emitted whether or not a bucket
// was ever provisioned — it is not evidence that one exists.) Nothing in the app
// uses Storage either: the iOS target links only FirebaseAuth and FirebaseCore,
// and no Swift or Cloud Functions source references FirebaseStorage.
//
// So these rules are currently unenforced and undeployed — `firebase deploy
// --only storage` would fail with no bucket to release them against. They exist
// to pin the intended posture for whenever Storage is turned on.
//
// Storage egress is billed on Blaze, and an open bucket is both a data leak and
// an unbounded invoice. Deny-by-default is the only safe posture for a bucket
// with no intended traffic — and it is deliberately *stricter* than the default
// ruleset Firebase installs when you first provision a bucket, which allows any
// authenticated user to read and write every object.
//
// When a feature does need Storage (profile avatars are the likely first one):
// enable the API, create the bucket, then add a narrowly scoped match above the
// catch-all — owner-keyed by uid, with an explicit size and contentType bound —
// and deploy. Relaxing the catch-all itself is never the right move.
service firebase.storage {
  match /b/{bucket}/o {
    match /{allPaths=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
